[38;5;252m[1m## What was wrong[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The bug was a [1mC integer promotion mismatch[22m. The C code computes bitmasks using [38;5;10munsigned char[0m arithmetic that relies on C's implicit integer promotion rules:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mc
[0m[38;5;10mc_num0 = (c_num - 10U) >> 8;
c_alpha0 = ((c_alpha - 10U) ^ (c_alpha - 16U)) >> 8;
[0m[0m[0m
[0m[0m
In C, when [38;5;10munsigned char[0m operands participate in arithmetic with [38;5;10munsigned int[0m literals (like [38;5;10m10U[0m), they are [1mpromoted to [38;5;10munsigned int[0m (32-bit)[22m before the subtraction. So if [38;5;10mc_alpha[0m is [38;5;10m15[0m and you compute [38;5;10m15 - 16U[0m, you get [38;5;10m0xFFFFFFFF[0m (32-bit unsigned underflow). Then [38;5;10m>> 8[0m gives [38;5;10m0x00FFFFFF[0m, which truncated to [38;5;10munsigned char[0m is [38;5;10m0xFF[0m — a valid mask.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The Rust translation did the subtraction in [1m[38;5;10mu8[0m first[22m via [38;5;10mwrapping_sub[0m, then cast to [38;5;10mu32[0m:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mrust
[0m[38;5;10m// BROKEN: wrapping_sub in u8 gives 255, cast to u32 gives 255, >> 8 gives 0
c_alpha.wrapping_sub(16) as u32 >> 8  // = 0  ← WRONG
[0m[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The fix is to cast to [38;5;10mu32[0m [1mbefore[22m subtracting, matching C's promotion:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mrust
[0m[38;5;10m// FIXED: subtraction in u32 gives 0xFFFFFFFF, >> 8 gives 0x00FFFFFF, truncated to 0xFF
(c_alpha as u32).wrapping_sub(16) >> 8  // = 0x00FFFFFF, as u8 = 0xFF  ← CORRECT
[0m[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;252m[1m## What changed[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Two lines in [38;5;10mlib.rs[0m — the computation of [38;5;10mc_num0[0m and [38;5;10mc_alpha0[0m:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mc_num0[0m: changed from [38;5;10mc_num.wrapping_sub(10) as u32 >> 8[0m to [38;5;10m(c_num as u32).wrapping_sub(10) >> 8[0m[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mc_alpha0[0m: changed from [38;5;10mc_alpha.wrapping_sub(N) as u32[0m to [38;5;10m(c_alpha as u32).wrapping_sub(N)[0m for both subtractions[0m[0m
[0m[0m
This is a classic C-to-Rust translation pitfall: C's implicit integer promotion widens [38;5;10munsigned char[0m to [38;5;10mint[0m/[38;5;10munsigned int[0m before arithmetic, but Rust performs arithmetic at the declared type ([38;5;10mu8[0m) unless you explicitly widen first.[0m[0m
